**Classification of all finite subgroups of \(GL_2(\mathbb{R})\)**

**Theorem.** Every finite subgroup of \(GL_2(\mathbb{R})\) is conjugate (inside \(GL_2(\mathbb{R})\)) to either a cyclic group \(C_n\) of order \(n\) or a dihedral group \(D_n\) of order \(2n\), for some integer \(n \geq 1\). Conversely, each of these occurs (up to conjugacy) as a finite subgroup of \(GL_2(\mathbb{R})\). Two such subgroups are conjugate in \(GL_2(\mathbb{R})\) iff they are isomorphic (i.e., same type and same \(n\)).

---

**Step 1. Reduction to \(O(2)\).**

Let \(G \leq GL_2(\mathbb{R})\) be finite. Define a new positive-definite symmetric bilinear form by averaging the standard dot product over \(G\):

\[
\langle v, w\rangle_G \;=\; \frac{1}{|G|}\sum_{A\in G} (Av)\cdot(Aw).
\]

This is positive definite (a sum of positive-definite forms is positive definite) and \(G\)-invariant: for \(B\in G\),

\[
\langle Bv,Bw\rangle_G = \frac{1}{|G|}\sum_{A\in G}(ABv)\cdot(ABw)=\frac{1}{|G|}\sum_{A'\in G}A'v\cdot A'w=\langle v,w\rangle_G,
\]

since left-multiplication by \(B\) permutes the elements of \(G\).

Choose a basis in which \(\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle_G\) is the standard dot product (Gram–Schmidt on this form). In that basis every element of \(G\) is orthogonal, i.e. \(G\) becomes a subgroup of \(O(2)\). Concretely, if \(P\) is the change-of-basis matrix, then \(PGP^{-1}\leq O(2)\). Hence **every finite subgroup of \(GL_2(\mathbb{R})\) is conjugate to a finite subgroup of \(O(2)\)**.

---

**Step 2. Finite subgroups of \(SO(2)\).**

\(SO(2)\) consists of rotation matrices

\[
R_\theta=\begin{pmatrix}\cos\theta&-\sin\theta\\ \sin\theta&\cos\theta\end{pmatrix},\qquad \theta\in\mathbb{R}/2\pi\mathbb{Z},
\]

and is isomorphic to the circle group \(S^1\) via \(\theta\mapsto R_\theta\). A finite subgroup of \(S^1\) consists of roots of unity: if \(R_\theta\) has finite order \(m\), then \(m\theta\equiv 0\pmod{2\pi}\), so \(\theta=2\pi k/m\). A finite subgroup is therefore generated by \(R_{2\pi/n}\) for some \(n\geq 1\), giving the cyclic group

\[
C_n=\langle R_{2\pi/n}\rangle=\{R_{2\pi k/n}:0\le k<n\},\qquad |C_n|=n.
\]

These are pairwise distinct for distinct \(n\).

---

**Step 3. Finite subgroups of \(O(2)\) not contained in \(SO(2)\).**

Let \(H\leq O(2)\) be finite with \(H\not\leq SO(2)\). The determinant map \(\det:H\to\{\pm1\}\) is a surjective homomorphism, so \(K:=H\cap SO(2)\) is a normal subgroup of index 2. By Step 2, \(K=C_n\) for some \(n\geq 1\), generated by \(r:=R_{2\pi/n}\).

Pick any \(s\in H\setminus SO(2)\); then \(\det s=-1\), so \(s\) is a reflection. Conjugating \(s\) by \(r\) gives another reflection \(rsr^{-1}\in H\). A direct computation shows that every reflection in \(O(2)\) is of the form \(R_\theta\,J\) where

\[
J=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&-1\end{pmatrix}
\]

is reflection in the \(x\)-axis, and \(R_\theta J = J R_{-\theta}\). In particular \(r=R_{2\pi/n}\) and \(s=J\) (after conjugating within \(O(2)\) we may take \(s=J\)) satisfy the dihedral relation

\[
srs^{-1}=r^{-1},\qquad s^2=1,\qquad r^n=1.
\]

Thus \(H=\langle r,s\rangle\) is presented by \(\langle r,s\mid r^n=s^2=1,\;srs=r^{-1}\rangle\), which is the **dihedral group \(D_n\)** of order \(2n\). Its elements are \(\{1,r,\dots,r^{n-1},s,rs,\dots,r^{n-1}s\}\), exactly \(n\) rotations and \(n\) reflections.

So every finite subgroup of \(O(2)\) is either \(C_n\) (inside \(SO(2)\)) or \(D_n\) (containing reflections), for some \(n\geq 1\).

---

**Step 4. Conjugacy classes in \(GL_2(\mathbb{R})\).**

Combining Steps 1 and 3: every finite subgroup of \(GL_2(\mathbb{R})\) is \(GL_2(\mathbb{R})\)-conjugate to either \(C_n\) or \(D_n\).

For distinctness: \(C_n\cong C_m\) iff \(n=m\); \(D_n\cong D_m\) iff \(n=m\) (for \(n,m\geq 3\); the small cases \(D_1\cong C_2\) and \(D_2\cong C_2\times C_2\) need care). Let me handle the small cases explicitly:

- \(n=1\): \(C_1=\{I\}\) (trivial), \(D_1=\langle J\rangle\cong C_2\) (a single reflection).
- \(n=2\): \(C_2=\langle -I\rangle\) (order 2, generated by central \(-I\)); \(D_2=\langle R_\pi,J\rangle=\{I,R_\pi,J,R_\pi J\}\cong C_2\times C_2\) (Klein four, generated by two perpendicular reflections).

The isomorphism types are all distinct except for the genuine coincidence \(D_1\cong C_2\): a group of order 2 generated by a reflection is abstractly \(C_2\), but as a *subgroup of \(GL_2(\mathbb{R})\) up to conjugacy* it matters whether the order-2 element has eigenvalues \(\{-1,-1\}\) (i.e. \(-I\), giving \(C_2\subset SO(2)\)) or eigenvalues \(\{+1,-1\}\) (a reflection, giving \(D_1\)). These two are **not** conjugate in \(GL_2(\mathbb{R})\) (different eigenvalue multisets), so they are genuinely different conjugacy classes, even though abstractly isomorphic. This is the standard subtlety, and it shows the classification is by *conjugacy class of embedding*, not merely by abstract isomorphism type.

More generally, \(C_n\) and \(D_n\) are never conjugate (one lies in \(SO(2)\) up to conjugacy, the other contains orientation-reversing elements — and the property "all elements have determinant \(+1\)" is conjugacy-invariant). And \(C_n\not\cong D_m\) for all \(n,m\) except the order-2 coincidence just discussed, while \(D_n,D_m\) for \(n,m\geq 2\) are non-conjugate because their rotation subgroups have different orders \(n\neq m\) (the order of the maximal cyclic normal subgroup is a conjugacy invariant).

---

**Conclusion.**

The finite subgroups of \(GL_2(\mathbb{R})\), up to conjugacy in \(GL_2(\mathbb{R})\), are exactly:

\[
\boxed{\,C_n\ (n\geq 1)\quad\text{and}\quad D_n\ (n\geq 1)\,}
\]

where \(C_n=\langle R_{2\pi/n}\rangle\) is the cyclic group of \(n\) rotations and \(D_n=\langle R_{2\pi/n},\,J\rangle\) is the dihedral group of order \(2n\) (rotations + reflections). Each is realized as a subgroup of \(O(2)\subset GL_2(\mathbb{R})\), every finite subgroup of \(GL_2(\mathbb{R})\) is conjugate to one of these (by averaging an inner product), and these classes are pairwise distinct as conjugacy classes of subgroups (with the noted subtlety that \(C_2\) and \(D_1\) are abstractly isomorphic but not conjugate, distinguished by whether the nontrivial element is \(-I\) or a reflection).

